-+**Why the harmonized series is VIIRS-referenced, and where MODIS enters.** The harmonized record is expressed on the VIIRS 375 m scale on purpose. A merged MODIS+VIIRS series needs one sensor's value to stand in for the other on days one is missing, and the data do not support that here: on the shipped Egypt data VIIRS totals are ~9x the k-scaled Aqua totals and correlate weakly with them (`daily_total_relationship`), because k is a ratio conditional on a MODIS detection and cannot recover the many small fires only VIIRS sees. MODIS therefore contributes four things, none of them a silent substitution: (1) the footprint-matched pixel table (`matched_pairs.csv`); (2) the per-satellite scale k with its CI, prediction interval and trust grade, i.e. the quantitative bridge between the two records; (3) MODIS expressed on the VIIRS scale with uncertainty (`modis_frp_viirs_equiv_mw`, `_lo_mw`, `_hi_mw`), so the pre-VIIRS / MODIS-only record can be read on the same axis; (4) `modis_only_evidence` on days VIIRS did not observe. Terra has no k against an afternoon VIIRS reference (~150 min apart) and so adds only detection counts; `PYRO_APPLY_UNVALIDATED_K=1` applies an unvalidated k and labels every value.
-+
-+**Time-gap window of k (J1 / NOAA-20).** `PYRO_K_MAX_DT_MIN` defaults to `auto`: matches within 30 minutes are used, and only if no MODIS satellite has enough of them the window is widened to 45, 60, 75 or 90 minutes (`PYRO_K_WIDEN_STEPS`). Aqua vs NOAA-20 is ~50 minutes apart, so with the J1 file you will see `k window: ... widened to 60 min`. A widened k is capped below HIGH trust and recorded in `frp_mapping.json` (`scale_policy.k_window`); part of its ratio is fire evolution between the overpasses. Set a number to fix the window.
